---
/**
 * WO-2.1 — /compass/what-is-inside
 * 301 target for compass.bearingbridge.com/en/what-you-can-find.
 *
 * The partner types, the nine profile field groups and the category coverage
 * list are ported. The "why we do not sell it as a product" framing is written
 * here in TheChinaPath's voice, and it does the work the spec's guardrail asks
 * for: Compass is how a shortlist gets built, not a subscription.
 */
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import {
  PARTNER_TYPES,
  PROFILE_FIELDS,
  CATEGORIES,
  CATEGORIES_CLOSING,
  APP_URL,
} from '../../data/compass';

const title = 'What is inside Compass | TheChinaPath';
const description =
  'Profiles of vetted China distributors, Tmall and Douyin Partners, importers and channel specialists. Real categories, real volume, real commercial terms, and our read on each one.';
---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>
  <section class="section section--dark cmp-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="eyebrow">What is inside</span>
      <h1 class="h1">Not names and email addresses.</h1>
      <p class="lead cmp-hero__lead">
        Distributors, Tmall Partners, Douyin Partners, importers and channel specialists across
        China. Each one gets a structured profile: the real categories they run, real volume,
        commercial terms, operational capacity and our team's read on whether to recommend them,
        and for what kind of brand.
      </p>
      <p class="cmp-hero__body">
        It is the kind of detail that actually decides whether a partner is the right fit, and it is
        the kind that never makes it into a pitch deck.
      </p>
      <div class="cmp-hero__cta">
        <a href="/compass/shortlist" class="btn btn--primary">Talk to us about your shortlist</a>
        <a href="/compass/how-it-works" class="btn btn--ghost">See how a shortlist is built</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Who is in it -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-header">
        <span class="eyebrow">Who is in it</span>
        <h2 class="h2">Distributors, TPs, importers and channel specialists across China.</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          Domestic and cross-border. Online and offline. Niche category specialists in the mix. A
          name only goes in when we have a real reason to know it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-4">
        {
          PARTNER_TYPES.map((t) => (
            <article class="card">
              <h3 class="card__title">{t.name}</h3>
              <p>{t.body}</p>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <div class="wii-coverage">
        <p class="wii-coverage__label">Categories covered</p>
        <div class="cmp-pills">
          {CATEGORIES.map((c) => <span class="cmp-pill">{c}</span>)}
        </div>
        <p class="wii-note">{CATEGORIES_CLOSING}</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- What we record -->
  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-header">
        <span class="eyebrow">What we record</span>
        <h2 class="h2">The fields that decide whether a partnership fits.</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          Nine groups of fields make up every profile. Together they cover the ground a sales deck
          rarely does, and the last one is the one clients read first.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-3">
        {
          PROFILE_FIELDS.map((f) => (
            <article class="card">
              <h3 class="card__title">{f.name}</h3>
              <p>{f.body}</p>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Why it is not a product -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container cmp-narrow">
      <span class="eyebrow">A fair question</span>
      <h2 class="h2">Why we do not sell you access to it.</h2>
      <p>
        People ask, reasonably, whether they can just have a login and browse. The answer is no, and
        the reason is not commercial. A profile is a judgement, and a judgement without the context
        behind it is worse than no information at all. "Recommend with caveats" means nothing until
        somebody tells you what the caveats are and whether they apply to a brand shaped like yours.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is a shelf-life problem too. A profile written eighteen months ago can already be
        wrong. Partners lose accounts, change categories, get bought, lose the one person who made
        them good. The work of a shortlist is half retrieval and half re-checking, and the
        re-checking is the part a subscription cannot do for you.
      </p>
      <p>
        So Compass is not a product with tiers. It is the thing our team works from when you brief
        us, and what you receive is the output of that work: a short list of partners, a written
        view on each, and a conversation about why. Partners who already have an account with us
        sign in at <a href={APP_URL}>compass.thechinapath.com</a>.
      </p>
      <div class="cmp-next">
        <a href="/compass/shortlist" class="btn btn--primary">Brief us on your category</a>
        <a href="/compass/how-it-works" class="btn btn--ghost">Next: how a shortlist is built</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .cmp-hero .h1 {
    max-width: 18ch;
  }
  .wii-coverage {
    margin-top: var(--space-7);
    padding-top: var(--space-5);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--bbg-border);
  }
  .wii-coverage__label {
    font-size: var(--fs-label);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--bbg-blue);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }
  .wii-note {
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
    color: var(--bbg-text-muted);
  }
</style>
